{
  "id": "fraud_detection",
  "title": "Real-time transaction fraud detection",
  "case_type": "fraud detection",
  "scenario": "Head of Fraud Risk: We want a fraud detection system that scores every card and account-to-account transaction for fraud before it is completed. Today we rely on fixed rules written years ago and lose a lot of money. We are not sure which signals will work, so we expect to try several machine-learning models and compare them on historical data before choosing one. Data science lead: Models need two years of labelled transaction history, device information and customer behaviour data. We will retrain the models regularly as fraud patterns change. Fraud analysts: Transactions with a high fraud score should be held and sent to an analyst queue; analysts confirm or release them, and their decisions feed back into training. We want fewer false alarms than today. Customer experience: Scoring must add no more than 100 milliseconds to a payment, and genuine customers should rarely be blocked. When a transaction is held, the customer should get an SMS asking them to confirm it. Compliance officer: Suspicious activity has to be reported to the financial intelligence unit, and we must be able to explain why a transaction was blocked if a customer complains. Every model version, its training data and its decisions must be recorded. Security team: Customer data used for training must be pseudonymised, and only the data-science team may access the training data. CIO: The budget for this project is fixed for this year and the first version must go live within six months. Every model must also be fully validated by the model-risk team before it touches production, which takes about three months per model.",
